// ═══ IDENTITY: DB auto-increment ═══
@Entity
public class Trade {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    // PostgreSQL: id BIGSERIAL (auto-increment)
    // MySQL: id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT
    // INSERT INTO trades (symbol, amount) VALUES ('RELIANCE', 245050)
    // → DB generates id=1, returns it → Hibernate reads it back
    // (Caution) DISABLES BATCH: Hibernate must flush EACH insert to get the ID!
    // 10,000 inserts: 10,000 round-trips. SLOW for bulk operations.
}

// ═══ SEQUENCE: DB sequence (RECOMMENDED for PostgreSQL) ═══
@Entity
public class Trade {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "trade_seq")
    @SequenceGenerator(name = "trade_seq", sequenceName = "trade_id_seq", allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;
    // Hibernate: SELECT nextval('trade_id_seq') → gets 50 IDs at once (allocationSize=50)
    // Assigns IDs 1-50 locally WITHOUT hitting DB for each insert
    // 10,000 inserts: only 200 sequence calls (10000/50) + batch INSERT
    // hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50 → INSERTs batched! 10x faster than IDENTITY.
}

// ═══ TABLE: key-generation table (slowest — avoid!) ═══
@Entity
public class Trade {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.TABLE, generator = "trade_table_gen")
    @TableGenerator(name = "trade_table_gen", table = "id_generator",
        pkColumnValue = "trade_id", allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;
    // Creates table: id_generator (gen_name VARCHAR, gen_value BIGINT)
    // SELECT gen_value FROM id_generator WHERE gen_name = 'trade_id' FOR UPDATE
    // → row-level lock! Only ONE thread can generate IDs at a time.
    // Portable across databases but SLOWEST strategy.
}

// ═══ AUTO: provider picks ═══
@Entity
public class Trade {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.AUTO)
    private Long id;
    // Hibernate decides:
    // PostgreSQL → SEQUENCE (has native sequences)
    // MySQL → IDENTITY (no native sequences pre-8.0) or TABLE
    // (Caution) Behavior may CHANGE between Hibernate versions!
    // Better to be EXPLICIT: use SEQUENCE or IDENTITY directly.
}

// ═══ UUID: application-generated ═══
@Entity
public class Trade {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)
    private UUID id;
    // Hibernate generates UUID in the JVM — NO DB round-trip for ID!
    // INSERT with ID already set → batch-friendly!
    // BUT: UUID is 16 bytes (vs 8 bytes bigint) → larger indexes
    //      Random UUID v4 → B-tree index fragmentation (random inserts)
    //      Fix: use UUIDv7 (time-ordered) → sequential inserts → good index perf
}
```

IDENTITY vs SEQUENCE — the batch insert difference:

```java
// ═══ WHY IDENTITY disables batching ═══
// Hibernate needs the generated ID IMMEDIATELY after each insert
// (to set it on the entity for the persistence context).
// With IDENTITY: the ID is generated BY the DB during INSERT.
// Hibernate MUST execute each INSERT individually to read back the ID.
// → Cannot batch multiple INSERTs into one JDBC call.

// SEQUENCE: Hibernate pre-fetches IDs BEFORE inserting.
// allocationSize=50: SELECT nextval('seq') once → 50 IDs available locally.
// Hibernate assigns IDs to 50 entities, then batch-INSERTs all 50 at once.
// hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50 + SEQUENCE = 10x faster than IDENTITY for bulk!

// BENCHMARK (10,000 inserts):
// IDENTITY:  10,000 individual INSERTs → ~15 seconds
// SEQUENCE:  200 sequence calls + 200 batch INSERTs (50 each) → ~1.5 seconds
// 10x speedup!
```

